The holidays are a wonderful time of year, but let's be honest: they can also be one of the most disruptive times for our health.

Between travel, family gatherings, extra commitments, late nights, different eating habits, and the pressure to get everything done, our normal routines can quickly go out the window.

And suddenly, we're feeling more tired, bloated, irritable, having trouble sleeping, experiencing more cravings, or noticing that our digestion just isn't where it normally is.

Sound familiar?

There's a reason for this. AND, there are things you can do about it!

Our bodies rely on consistency. The everyday habits that support digestion, blood sugar, hormones, and the nervous system are often the first to slip when life gets busy.

When we skip meals, rely on caffeine to get through the day, eat at irregular times, move less, sleep less, and operate under more stress, these small changes can add up.

### Here's What May Be Happening

-   **Blood sugar becomes less stable.** Skipping meals, eating irregularly, and relying on sweets or refined carbohydrates can contribute to energy fluctuations, increased cravings, and feeling hungry throughout the day.
-   **Stress levels increase.** More demands on our time can mean less opportunity to rest and recover. Chronic stress can influence cortisol patterns, digestion, sleep, and blood sugar regulation.
-   **Digestion slows down.** Changes in meal timing, food choices, hydration, and movement can affect bowel regularity and digestive comfort.
-   **Sleep takes a hit.** Late nights, alcohol, heavier meals, and disrupted routines can interfere with sleep quality, leaving us feeling less rested and more depleted.
-   **We lose the habits that keep us feeling our best.** Exercise, time outside, balanced meals, and moments of downtime often become less of a priority.

![](/images/articles/holiday-health-habits-1.webp)

### Why Foundational Habits Matter Most

This is exactly why I talk so much about the foundations of health.

Before adding more supplements or complicated protocols, it's important to make sure the basics are in place.

Our bodies need consistent nourishment, adequate protein, hydration and minerals, regular movement, restorative sleep, and time to recover from stress.

These habits may not seem particularly exciting, but they provide the building blocks for so many of the processes that keep us feeling well.

And here's the good news: You don't need to do everything perfectly to make a difference.

### 5 Small Habits to Prioritize This Holiday Season

**1. Start your day with protein.**

Rather than relying on coffee, carbs, or skipping breakfast, start with a protein-rich meal. This anchors your entire day and helps support blood sugar balance, sustained energy, and appetite regulation.

**2. Stay hydrated and replenish minerals.**

Don't let hydration fall to the bottom of your to-do list. Drink water consistently throughout the day, add sea salt, lemon, coconut water, or quality electrolytes.

**3. Keep digestion moving.**

Slow down when eating, chew thoroughly, and take a short walk after meals when you can. Prioritize fiber, like flax and chia.

These simple habits can help support digestive function and regularity.

**4. Protect your sleep.**

Try to maintain a relatively consistent bedtime, get morning sunlight, and give yourself time to wind down at night. Sleep is one of the most important tools we have for recovery and overall health. When all else is hitting the fan, sleep should be the one thing you protect the most.

**5. Make time for movement and recovery.**

You don't need an intense workout to support your health. Walking, stretching, and getting outside all count. Just as important, make room for downtime.

![](/images/articles/holiday-health-habits-2.webp)

### A Little Reminder...

You don't have to choose between enjoying the holidays and taking care of your health.

You don't need to avoid every holiday treat, turn down invitations, or follow a rigid routine. Instead, focus on maintaining a few foundational habits that help your body feel supported.

Remember, consistency matters more than perfection. And sometimes, the simplest things we do every day have the biggest impact on how we feel.

Enjoy the holidays, take care of yourself, and give your body the support it needs!
